{"from": "text:euripides-bacchae", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-26-14-goads-proverb", "grade": "A", "note": "Euripides, Bacchae 794-795: Dionysus to Pentheus, 'Better to yield him prayer and sacrifice than kick against the pricks, since Dionyse is God, and thou but mortal' (Greek: pros kentra laktizoimi thnetos on theo) - the proverb's existence in Greek is exactly the claim."}
{"from": "text:plato-gorgias", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-26-26-corner-idiom", "grade": "C", "note": "Callicles on philosophers: 'they avoid the busy haunts of men, and skulk in corners' (Gorgias 485d-e) - the same Greek idiom of corner-seclusion versus public life, three centuries earlier."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-162", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-26-05-pharisee-strictest", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: 'the Pharisees are those who are esteemed most skillful in the exact explication of their laws' - the sect's superlative reputation for exactness (akribeia) in the same period and region; it does not name Paul, so B not A."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-1-110", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-26-05-pharisee-strictest", "grade": "C", "note": "'a certain sect of the Jews that appear more religious than others, and seem to interpret the laws more accurately' - a comparative, earlier notice of the same reputation."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-13", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-26-06-08-resurrection-hope", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: the Pharisees believe 'the former shall have power to revive and live again' - the same sect's resurrection doctrine, the hope Paul says the twelve tribes serve night and day to attain."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-15-44", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-26-28-christian-name", "grade": "B", "note": "Tacitus: 'Christus, from whom the name had its origin' - the name Christianoi/Chrestianoi in Roman use from Nero's reign onward; it does not name Agrippa's exchange, so B."}
{"from": "text:suetonius-nero-16", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-26-28-christian-name", "grade": "B", "note": "'Punishment was inflicted on the Christians, a class of men given to a new and mischievous superstition' - the name in currency under Nero, the emperor before whom Paul's appeal would be heard."}
{"from": "text:pliny-epistles-10-96", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-26-28-christian-name", "grade": "B", "note": "Pliny's trials asked whether 'the \"name\" of Christianity itself' sufficed to condemn, or only the crimes attached to the name - the name as a recognized charge in the period."}
